/* Lessons content — stages 1-2. Schema: intro, question, analogy, analogyFail, explain[], tech[], code, codeNotes[], output, why, lang, interactive, sim, mistakes[], practices[], quiz[], takeaways[], recall, todo, anim{title,steps[]} */
export const LESSONS_A={
'prog-what':{intro:'You already give instructions every day — to friends, to delivery apps, to a microwave. Programming is writing instructions so precisely that even a very fast but utterly literal computer can follow them with zero guessing.',question:'If you had a robot helper that takes everything literally, how carefully would you word “make tea”?',analogy:'A recipe card. Ingredients = data, steps = instructions, cook = computer. If a step says “add some salt”, ten cooks make ten different dishes — and a computer, which cannot taste, just fails.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: a human cook guesses your intent and a kitchen tolerates mess. A computer never guesses and never cleans up after vague steps — imprecision becomes a crash or a wrong answer, not a quirky meal.',explain:['A <b>program</b> is a list of exact instructions plus the data they work on. Your music app, bank app, and games are all programs — millions of tiny exact steps each.','Every program follows <b>input → process → output</b>. Input arrives from taps, files, sensors, or the network. Processing calculates, decides, and repeats. Output leaves through the screen, speakers, files, or the network.','You write programs in a <b>programming language</b> — a small, strict language with no small talk and no synonyms. Java is one of the most widely used, especially for banks, shops, and Android apps.','One program can run many times on different data. The addition program below adds 4 + 5 today and 100 + 200 tomorrow — the instructions stay fixed, the data changes. That split is the whole idea.'],tech:['A running program is called a <b>process</b>; the saved file is the program. Same recipe, many separate cookings.','<b>Software</b> = programs + data + configuration. An <b>app</b> is just a program with a friendly face.','Computers only execute <b>machine code</b> (numbers the CPU understands). Everything you write gets translated into it — in Java, in two stages you will learn next.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int a = 4;              // input data\n    int b = 5;              // input data\n    int sum = a + b;        // process: add\n    System.out.println(sum); // output: 9\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['Lines 3–4 store the input data in named boxes.','Line 5 processes: it adds the two stored values.','Line 6 outputs the result to the screen.'],output:'9',why:'The computer did not “understand” addition the way you do. It executed the + instruction on the two stored values (4 and 5) and printed whatever came out: 9.',sim:{kind:'arith',label:'Change the numbers and operator — the computer just follows orders.',fields:[{v:'4'},{o:['+','-','*','/']},{v:'5'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'“The computer will figure out what I mean.”',why:'Computers take everything literally. “Sort of 5” is meaningless to them.',fix:'Say exactly what to do: add 5, print the result, stop.'},{bad:'Thinking data and instructions are the same thing.',why:'Data (4, 5) sits still. Instructions (+, print) act on it. Mixing them up makes programs unreadable.',fix:'Label each line: is this storing, doing, or showing?'},{bad:'Starting with a giant app idea like “a social network”.',why:'Big programs are hundreds of small instruction lists combined. Beginners drown starting at the top.',fix:'Start with input → one step → output. Grow from there.'}],practices:[{title:'Label the parts',task:'Look at the code above. Next to each line write I (input/data), P (process), or O (output).',hint:'Lines with = store data. The + line processes. println shows.',sol:'int a / int b = data/input; a + b = process; println = output.'},{title:'Write a 5-step robot recipe',task:'Write exact steps for “brush teeth” as if for a literal robot. No step may require guessing.',hint:'Include amounts, order, and when to stop.',sol:'Example: 1 pick up brush. 2 add pea-size paste. 3 brush top teeth 30 seconds. 4 brush bottom 30 seconds. 5 rinse 5 seconds, stop.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'What is a program?',options:[{t:'Exact instructions plus the data they work on',ok:true},{t:'A vague idea the computer guesses at'},{t:'Only the screen you see'},{t:'A physical computer part'}],explain:'A program = precise instructions operating on data. Remove either half and nothing runs.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'In input → process → output, “print the sum” is…',options:[{t:'Output',ok:true},{t:'Input'},{t:'Storage device'},{t:'Compiler'}],explain:'Printing shows a result to the outside world, so it is output.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'Why must instructions be exact?',options:[{t:'Computers follow them literally and never guess',ok:true},{t:'Computers get bored otherwise'},{t:'Exact code runs on paper only'},{t:'It makes files smaller'}],explain:'Literal execution is the entire reason precision matters.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'Same addition program, new numbers 100 + 200. What changes?',options:[{t:'Only the data; the instructions stay fixed',ok:true},{t:'You must rewrite the whole program'},{t:'The computer learns a new operation'},{t:'Nothing can change after writing'}],explain:'Programs are reusable: fixed instructions, changing data.'}],takeaways:['A program = exact instructions + data.','Input → process → output explains every app.','Computers never guess; precision is kindness to them.','Fixed instructions run many times on different data.'],recall:'Say in one sentence what a program does with input.',todo:'Pick one app you used today and name its input, process, and output.',anim:{title:'Recipe becomes program',steps:[{caption:'A recipe lists ingredients (data).',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>Ingredients</b><br>flour · eggs · 180°C</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>Steps</b><br>mix → bake → cool</div></div>'},{caption:'A program lists data the same way.',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">a</div><div class="vl">4</div></div><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">b</div><div class="vl">5</div></div></div>'},{caption:'Then instructions act on it, and output appears.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>Process</b><br>a + b = 9</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>Output</b><br>print 9</div></div>'}]}},
'algorithm':{intro:'An algorithm is a recipe strict enough for a machine: finite steps, a clear order, and a definite end. Every program you will ever write is one or more algorithms.',question:'How would you teach a friend who has never been to your street to reach your door, using only a phone call?',analogy:'Morning routine: alarm → brush → dress → eat. Same order daily, ends when you leave. A loop-free algorithm feels exactly like this — steps you could chant.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: “get dressed nicely” works for humans and fails for machines. “Nicely” is an opinion, not a step. Algorithms need testable steps like “put on the blue shirt”.',explain:['An <b>algorithm</b> takes input, follows clear steps in order, and ends with output. “Sort these 5 cards” with written steps qualifies; “be creative” does not.','Good steps are <b>unambiguous</b> (exactly one meaning), <b>ordered</b> (step 2 needs only steps before it), and <b>finite</b> (they always stop). Miss any one and it is not an algorithm.','Programmers judge algorithms by correctness first, then by step count: does it always give the right answer, and roughly how many steps does it take as input grows? That “how many steps” idea is called <b>complexity</b>, and you will feel it with loops and searching later.','Algorithms live above any language. The max-of-two idea below works identically in Java, Python, or pseudocode — only the spelling changes.'],tech:['An algorithm must terminate for <b>every</b> allowed input, not just the examples you tried. “Keep guessing” is not an algorithm.','Steps must be <b>effective</b>: simple enough that the follower can actually do them. “Teleport the cheese” is unambiguous but not effective.','Writing the steps in plain words before coding is called <b>pseudocode</b> — the professional habit this lesson is really teaching.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int a = 7;   // input\n    int b = 4;   // input\n    int max;     // will hold the answer\n    if (a > b) {\n      max = a;\n    } else {\n      max = b;\n    }\n    System.out.println(max); // output: 7\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['Steps run in order: store, compare, pick, print.','The if is a decision point — still an exact step.','It always ends after printing: finite.'],output:'7',why:'Compare 7 > 4: true, so max = 7. The else branch is skipped. Any two numbers reach the print — finite and exact.',sim:{kind:'cond',label:'Try values: the algorithm picks a branch and always finishes.',fields:[{v:'7'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'“Step 3: do the needful.”',why:'Vague verbs cannot execute on any machine.',fix:'Replace with a measurable action: “add 1 spoon of sugar, stir 10 times.”'},{bad:'Forgetting the stop condition (“keep stirring”).',why:'An endless procedure is not an algorithm and bakes a real program into a frozen one.',fix:'Add an end: “stir 10 times, then stop.”'},{bad:'Steps in the wrong order (“bake, then mix”).',why:'Later steps need results that do not exist yet.',fix:'Number the steps; check each one needs only earlier results.'}],practices:[{title:'Spot the vague step',task:'Which is NOT a good algorithm step? A) “add 200 g flour” B) “bake 20 min at 180 °C” C) “make it tasty”. Rewrite the bad one.',hint:'Can a robot test whether it did it right?',sol:'C is not testable. Rewrite: “add 1 tsp salt”.'},{title:'Write min-of-three',task:'Write at most 6 steps to find the smallest of x, y, z.',hint:'Compare two, keep the winner, compare with the third.',sol:'1 set m = x. 2 if y < m set m = y. 3 if z < m set m = z. 4 output m.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'What must an algorithm have?',options:[{t:'Clear ordered steps and a guaranteed end',ok:true},{t:'At least 100 steps'},{t:'A computer to run on'},{t:'Fancy words'}],explain:'Finite, ordered, unambiguous steps — the three non-negotiables.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'“Keep guessing until it feels right” fails because…',options:[{t:'It has no definite end',ok:true},{t:'It is too short'},{t:'Guessing is illegal'},{t:'It uses numbers'}],explain:'No testable stop means it is a habit, not an algorithm.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'The code above with a = 2, b = 9 prints…',options:[{t:'9',ok:true},{t:'2'},{t:'0'},{t:'It never ends'}],explain:'9 > 2 is false… wait: a=2, b=9, so (2 > 9) is false → else branch → max = b = 9.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'Why write pseudocode before real code?',options:[{t:'To fix the logic while it is still cheap to change',ok:true},{t:'Compilers require it'},{t:'It runs faster'},{t:'It replaces testing'}],explain:'Logic errors found in plain words cost seconds; found in code cost much more.'}],takeaways:['Algorithm = exact steps + order + guaranteed end.','Each step must be testable by a literal follower.','Order matters; each step uses only earlier results.','Pseudocode first, code second — always.'],recall:'What three properties make steps “algorithm-quality”?',todo:'Write sandwich-making steps (max 8) a robot could follow, then test them on a human robot.',anim:{title:'Vague vs exact',steps:[{caption:'Vague step: the robot freezes.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>Step</b><br>“make it nice”</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>Robot</b><br>⁉️ which action?</div></div>'},{caption:'Exact steps: the robot flows.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>1</b> add 200 g flour</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>2</b> bake 20 min</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>3</b> stop</div></div>'},{caption:'Same idea in code: compare, pick, print, stop.',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">max</div><div class="vl">7</div></div></div>'}]}},
'source-code':{intro:'Source code is the human-readable text you write and edit. The computer later translates it — but humans must read it first, including future you at 2 a.m.',question:'Why do chefs write recipes down instead of just filming themselves cooking once?',analogy:'A recipe book versus the meal. The book (source) can be shared, reviewed, corrected, and re-cooked a thousand times. The meal (the running app) disappears after eating.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: you can still eat without the recipe. A computer cannot run untranslated source — the text alone does nothing until it is built. Source is potential, not dinner.',explain:['<b>Source code</b> is plain-text instructions in a language like Java. Java files end in <b>.java</b> and contain classes, methods, and statements.','Code obeys strict <b>syntax</b> (spelling and grammar). One missing semicolon can stop the whole translation — the translator has no sense of humor about it.','You always write for two readers: the translator, which is strict but dumb, and humans, who need clear names, layout, and comments. Professionals optimize for the humans.','Code is edited far more than it is written. A typical file is read hundreds of times per change, which is why readability rules in this course are not decoration.'],tech:['Java source is <b>Unicode text</b>; the compiler reads it and produces <b>bytecode</b> files ending in <b>.class</b>.','The compiler checks syntax <b>and</b> types before producing anything — a file with one error yields no runnable output at all.','By convention, one <b>public</b> class per file, and the file name matches it exactly: <b>Hello</b> lives in <b>Hello.java</b>.'],code:`// Hello.java — source code (human readable)\npublic class Hello {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println("Hi, future programmer!");\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['Line 1 is a comment — for humans only, ignored by the translator.','Lines 2–3 wrap the program in a class plus its starting point.','Line 4 is the one real instruction.'],output:'Hi, future programmer!',why:'The translator keeps line 4 as an instruction and discards the comment. Running the result prints the text.',sim:{kind:'concat',label:'Source text builds output text. Try joining words.',fields:[{v:'Hi, '},{v:'future programmer!'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'Trying to fix the running app instead of the code.',why:'The running app is output; the cause lives in source. Patching output fixes nothing.',fix:'Change the .java file, rebuild, run again.'},{bad:'Ignoring one red semicolon error.',why:'Translation is all-or-nothing: one error, zero program.',fix:'Read the FIRST error line, fix exactly that, rebuild.'},{bad:'Names like a1, x2, tmp everywhere.',why:'Compiles fine, reads terribly — and code is read far more than written.',fix:'Use total, userName, price.'}],practices:[{title:'Find the syntax break',task:'Which line stops translation? A) // hi B) System.out.println("hi") C) System.out.println("hi");',hint:'Java statements end with a semicolon.',sol:'B is missing its semicolon. C is correct; A is a comment and always safe.'},{title:'Rename well',task:'Rename int a1 (the price of apples) to a good name.',hint:'Say what it IS, in full words.',sol:'int applePrice; — future you says thanks.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'Source code is…',options:[{t:'Human-readable text the translator builds from',ok:true},{t:'The running app itself'},{t:'Electricity in wires'},{t:'A password'}],explain:'Source is the recipe text; the app is the meal.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'Java source files end with…',options:[{t:'.java',ok:true},{t:'.exe'},{t:'.jpg'},{t:'.txt only'}],explain:'By convention and tooling: .java in, .class out.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'Why do comments exist?',options:[{t:'To explain why, for humans; the translator ignores them',ok:true},{t:'To make code run faster'},{t:'They are required on every line'},{t:'To encrypt code'}],explain:'Comments help readers and never change behavior.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'One error in a 500-line file means…',options:[{t:'No runnable program until it is fixed',ok:true},{t:'The other 499 lines still run'},{t:'Only that line is skipped'},{t:'The error line runs twice'}],explain:'Compilation is all-or-nothing.'}],takeaways:['Source code is text humans write, read, and edit.','Syntax must be exact; comments are free notes.','Name things for humans, not just the machine.','One public class per file, file name matches it.'],recall:'Who are the two readers of source code, and what does each want?',todo:'Open Hello.java and add a comment with your name and today’s date.',anim:{title:'Source → translator → app',steps:[{caption:'You write source (Hello.java).',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>Hello.java</b><br>println("Hi")</div></div>'},{caption:'The translator checks syntax strictly.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>javac</b><br>missing ; ? STOP</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>Hello.class</b><br>bytecode</div></div>'},{caption:'Running shows the app.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>Run</b><br>java Hello</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>Output</b><br>Hi!</div></div>'}]}},
'execute':{intro:'Your code never runs directly. It is fetched, decoded, and executed — billions of tiny steps per second — with memory as the workbench. Understand this and half of all future errors make sense.',question:'If a chef can hold only one recipe card at a time, how does the kitchen still produce a feast?',analogy:'Kitchen: recipe shelf (disk storage), counter (RAM memory), chef hands (CPU). Ingredients travel shelf → counter → hands, one card at a time, blindingly fast.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: chefs improvise, multitask, and remember. A CPU core does exactly one tiny operation per tick with zero memory of intent — all the speed comes from billions of ticks per second, not cleverness.',explain:['The <b>CPU</b> repeats one cycle: <b>fetch</b> the next instruction, <b>decode</b> what it means, <b>execute</b> it. A 3 GHz CPU does this roughly 3 billion times per second.','<b>RAM (memory)</b> holds the running code and its data — extremely fast, but erased when power goes. <b>Disk/SSD</b> keeps files for years but is far slower. Running = copy from disk to RAM, then the CPU works from RAM.','<b>Machine code</b> — pure numbers — is all the CPU truly understands. Java compiles to an intermediate <b>bytecode</b>, and the JVM converts that into machine steps on the fly.','This is why a “stuck” program still burns CPU (it is executing — just the wrong steps) and why unsaved work vanishes in a blackout (it lived only in RAM).'],tech:['Fetch–decode–execute is called the <b>instruction cycle</b>; each round handles one machine instruction, not one line of Java.','RAM is <b>volatile</b> (loses everything without power); SSD/disk is <b>persistent</b>. Save = copy RAM content to disk.','A program’s bytecode is platform-independent; the JVM is platform-specific — that split is what makes “write once, run anywhere” possible.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int a = 4;              // LOAD 4 into memory slot a\n    int b = 5;              // LOAD 5 into memory slot b\n    int c = a + b;          // ADD the slots into c\n    System.out.println(c);  // PRINT c  -> shows 9\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['Fetch: the CPU grabs the next instruction from RAM.','Decode: it figures out LOAD vs ADD vs PRINT.','Execute: it does it, then moves to the next line.'],output:'9',why:'Four trivial machine steps — load, load, add, print — combine into “add and print”. Enormous speed × extreme simplicity = all computing.',sim:{kind:'arith',label:'Each tiny step is exact. Change values, watch one ADD.',fields:[{v:'4'},{o:['+']},{v:'5'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'“RAM and disk are the same thing.”',why:'RAM is the counter (fast, temporary); disk is the shelf (slow, permanent). Confusing them makes “where did my file go” mysteries.',fix:'Running data lives in RAM; saved files live on disk.'},{bad:'“The CPU understands Java.”',why:'It understands machine numbers only. Java reaches it through two translations.',fix:'Java source → bytecode → machine steps, via the JVM.'},{bad:'“Shorter code always runs faster.”',why:'One short line can hide a million machine steps (a loop); ten long lines can be ten steps.',fix:'Count what the machine DOES, not lines you see.'}],practices:[{title:'Order the cycle',task:'Put in order: A) execute B) fetch C) decode.',hint:'You must collect it before you can read it.',sol:'B fetch → C decode → A execute.'},{title:'RAM vs disk',task:'A running variable lives where? The Hello.java file lives where? What survives a blackout?',hint:'Counter vs shelf.',sol:'Variable in RAM; Hello.java on disk; only the disk copy survives.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'The CPU cycle order is…',options:[{t:'Fetch → decode → execute',ok:true},{t:'Execute → fetch → decode'},{t:'Decode → save → sleep'},{t:'Print → fetch → eat'}],explain:'Collect it, understand it, do it — billions of times per second.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'RAM is like…',options:[{t:'A kitchen counter: fast workspace, wiped after',ok:true},{t:'A recipe book kept on the shelf'},{t:'The chef’s hat'},{t:'A printer'}],explain:'Fast, temporary working space. Power off = wiped clean.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'What does the CPU directly run?',options:[{t:'Machine code (numbers)',ok:true},{t:'Java source text'},{t:'English recipes'},{t:'Comments'}],explain:'Everything must become machine steps before the CPU touches it.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'Your program freezes but the fan spins loudly. Likely…',options:[{t:'It is executing — probably the wrong steps (e.g. an endless loop)',ok:true},{t:'The CPU has stopped working'},{t:'RAM has been deleted'},{t:'The screen is broken'}],explain:'Heat + noise = work happening. Infinite loops look exactly like this.'}],takeaways:['CPU: fetch → decode → execute, billions per second.','RAM = fast temporary counter; disk = long-term shelf.','Java runs via bytecode + JVM, never directly on CPU.','Frozen + fan = still executing (wrong steps).'],recall:'Name the three CPU steps and what each does.',todo:'Explain fetch–decode–execute to someone in 30 seconds, using the kitchen.',anim:{title:'Fetch-decode-execute',steps:[{caption:'1 FETCH: grab the next card from the counter.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>Memory</b><br>ADD A,B</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>CPU</b><br>got it</div></div>'},{caption:'2 DECODE: what does ADD mean?',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>CPU</b><br>ADD = combine numbers</div></div>'},{caption:'3 EXECUTE: do it, store 9, next card.',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">C</div><div class="vl">9</div></div></div>'}]}},
'java-intro':{intro:'Java runs banks, Android apps, Minecraft, and half the big websites you use. You are learning it because it is strict in a helpful way — the compiler coaches you before users suffer.',question:'Why would a beginner deliberately pick a language that forces extra rules?',analogy:'Training wheels with a city map. Java’s rules feel fussy at first — like bike lanes — but they prevent whole categories of crashes and the map leads downtown: jobs, Android, servers.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: training wheels never win races. Java is verbose, and a 3-line chore is genuinely faster in Python. Java wins at big, long-lived programs worked on by teams.',explain:['<b>Java</b> appeared in 1995 at Sun Microsystems (now Oracle; free OpenJDK builds exist). It is general-purpose, <b>object-oriented</b>, and <b>statically typed</b> — you declare each variable’s type, so whole bug families are caught before running.','Java builds Android apps, bank and shop servers, desktop tools, Minecraft, and big-data systems. Wherever programs must run for years without rotting, Java shows up.','Why learn it first: the strict compiler teaches discipline that transfers everywhere; the job market is enormous; and its ideas carry straight into C#, Kotlin, and TypeScript.','This course targets <b>Java 17 (LTS)</b>: a Long-Term-Support release, modern but stable, with free updates for years. Old tutorials teach Java 8 habits — you will learn the modern way.'],tech:['<b>Statically typed</b> = every variable’s type is fixed at compile time. <b>Object-oriented</b> = programs model things as objects with data + behavior (Stage 5).','<b>WORA</b> (“write once, run anywhere”): compiled bytecode runs on any machine with a matching JVM — Windows, Mac, Linux, servers.','<b>LTS</b> releases (8, 11, 17, 21) get years of free security fixes; this course pins Java 17 so examples behave identically for everyone.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println("Java runs everywhere!");\n    System.out.println("Strict today, grateful tomorrow.");\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['The same class + main uniform as every Java app you will write.','Two println instructions run top-down.','Curly braces group the code that belongs together.'],output:'Java runs everywhere!\nStrict today, grateful tomorrow.',why:'The JVM runs this same bytecode on any operating system — the promise of write-once-run-anywhere in two lines.',sim:{kind:'concat',label:'Java strings join with +. Try it.',fields:[{v:'Java '},{v:'runs everywhere!'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'“Java and JavaScript are the same thing.”',why:'Different languages sharing only a name (a 1990s marketing accident).',fix:'Java = big applications; JavaScript = mostly web pages.'},{bad:'Expecting zero rules and fighting every error.',why:'Java’s rules ARE the teaching: types, semicolons, braces.',fix:'Treat each compiler error as free coaching, not failure.'},{bad:'Following a 2014 Java 8 tutorial by default.',why:'Old idioms linger online and teach around features you should use.',fix:'We target Java 17 LTS — modern, supported, stable.'}],practices:[{title:'Where is Java?',task:'Name 3 places Java runs: a phone thing, a money thing, a game thing.',hint:'Think Android, bank servers, Minecraft.',sol:'Any three: Android apps, bank servers, Minecraft, web back ends, university courses.'},{title:'Strict is helpful — how?',task:'Give one concrete benefit of declaring types up front.',hint:'When is the cheapest moment to catch a mistake?',sol:'The compiler rejects “apple + 5” in seconds, before any user ever suffers.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'Java is best described as…',options:[{t:'General-purpose, object-oriented, statically typed',ok:true},{t:'Only for web pages'},{t:'The same as JavaScript'},{t:'A computer brand'}],explain:'That trio is Java’s identity; each word rules out a misconception.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'“Write once, run anywhere” works because…',options:[{t:'The same bytecode runs on many systems via each system’s JVM',ok:true},{t:'You only ever write code one time'},{t:'No installation is needed anywhere'},{t:'It runs without computers'}],explain:'The JVM absorbs the differences between machines.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'Which job fits Java LEAST?',options:[{t:'A tiny throwaway browser animation',ok:true},{t:'An Android app'},{t:'A bank back end'},{t:'Minecraft-style game'}],explain:'Tiny web scripts favor JavaScript; Java shines at big, long-lived systems.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'This course targets Java 17 LTS mainly because…',options:[{t:'It is modern, stable, and supported for years',ok:true},{t:'It is the only Java that exists'},{t:'Older versions cannot print'},{t:'LTS means fastest'}],explain:'LTS = long-term security fixes; 17 = modern features without churn.'}],takeaways:['Java = strict, object-oriented, runs everywhere.','Powers Android, servers, games, tools.','We use modern Java 17 LTS — ignore Java 8-era tutorials.'],recall:'What does “statically typed” mean, in one line?',todo:'Ask a friend to name one app; argue whether Java could build it and why.',anim:{title:'Write once, run anywhere',steps:[{caption:'You write one Main.java.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>Main.java</b><br>one source</div></div>'},{caption:'The compiler makes bytecode (.class).',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>javac</b></div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>Main.class</b><br>bytecode</div></div>'},{caption:'Each system’s JVM runs it unchanged.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">Windows JVM</div><div class="box">Mac JVM</div><div class="box">Linux JVM</div></div>'}]}},
'jdk-jre-jvm':{intro:'Three letters, one pipeline: the JDK builds, the JRE runs, the JVM executes. Install the JDK and you quietly get all three.',question:'On a film set, who writes the script, who owns the studio, and who actually acts?',analogy:'Script tools (JDK) → equipped studio (JRE) → actor (JVM) performing your scene. The writer needs the studio; the audience only needs the studio + actor.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: real studios differ per film. JVMs are deliberately near-identical — the same bytecode behaves the same on every system. That sameness is the product.',explain:['The <b>JVM</b> (Java Virtual Machine) executes bytecode and manages memory. There is one per platform, all behaving the same.','The <b>JRE</b> (Runtime Environment) is JVM + the standard libraries needed to run apps — for users, no compiler inside.','The <b>JDK</b> (Development Kit) is JRE + the <b>javac</b> compiler + tools like <b>java</b> and <b>jar</b> — for developers. Install a JDK 17 and you cover everything.','Your daily loop: <b>javac</b> translates <b>.java → .class</b>; <b>java</b> launches the JVM on that class. An error at the first step is a code problem; at the second, a runtime problem.'],tech:['<b>javac</b> exists only in the JDK. If your terminal says “javac not found”, you installed a JRE (or nothing), not a JDK.','<b>java -version</b> and <b>javac -version</b> should both report 17 — mismatched pairs cause confusing errors.','Free JDK 17 builds: Temurin (Eclipse), Oracle OpenJDK, Amazon Corretto — all run this course identically.'],code:`$ javac Main.java   # JDK: source -> Main.class\n$ java Main         # JRE/JVM: run it, prints Hi\nHi`,codeNotes:['javac only exists in the JDK — it compiles.','java launches using the JRE + JVM — it runs.','First line builds; second line executes.'],output:'Hi',why:'Compile once with the JDK; then any machine’s JVM executes the bytecode. Two tools, two jobs.',lang:'bash',sim:{kind:'concat',label:'No simulation — remember: JDK contains JRE contains JVM.',fields:[{v:'JDK '},{v:'includes JRE + JVM'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'Installing only the JRE to learn coding.',why:'A JRE cannot compile anything — you will stare at “javac not found”.',fix:'Install a JDK 17 (Temurin or Oracle) instead.'},{bad:'“The JVM is the compiler.”',why:'javac compiles; the JVM runs. Swapping the words guarantees confusion later.',fix:'javac = build, java = run.'},{bad:'Mixing a Java 8 javac with a Java 17 java (or reverse).',why:'Old builders + new runners (or reverse) produce bizarre version errors.',fix:'Check java -version and javac -version: both should say 17.'}],practices:[{title:'Who does what?',task:'Match each to its job: A) javac B) java C) JVM — compiles / launches / executes.',hint:'Build → launch → execute.',sol:'A compiles source, B launches the runtime, C executes bytecode.'},{title:'Which package for whom?',task:'You want to WRITE Java. Your friend only wants to RUN your app. What does each install?',hint:'Coders need the compiler; players need the player.',sol:'You: JDK. Friend: JRE (a JDK works too, just heavier).'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'Which bundle contains the compiler?',options:[{t:'JDK',ok:true},{t:'JRE only'},{t:'JVM only'},{t:'None of them'}],explain:'JDK = JRE + javac + developer tools.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'What actually executes bytecode?',options:[{t:'The JVM',ok:true},{t:'Notepad'},{t:'javac'},{t:'The .java file itself'}],explain:'The JVM executes and manages memory while running.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'javac Main.java reports errors. That means…',options:[{t:'A compile-time problem — fix the code, rebuild',ok:true},{t:'The computer is broken forever'},{t:'The internet is down'},{t:'The output is correct'}],explain:'javac errors always mean: fix the source first, then re-run.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'“javac: command not found” most likely means…',options:[{t:'No JDK on PATH (JRE-only or nothing installed)',ok:true},{t:'Your code has a typo'},{t:'Java is too old'},{t:'The file is too big'}],explain:'The tool itself is missing — an install problem, not a code problem.'}],takeaways:['JVM runs, JRE runs-with-libraries, JDK builds.','Install JDK 17; both version commands should say 17.','javac builds; java runs. Errors tell you which stage broke.'],recall:'What does each letter-group give you that the smaller one does not?',todo:'Run java -version and javac -version in a terminal and write down both numbers.',anim:{title:'JDK contains JRE contains JVM',steps:[{caption:'JVM: the actor executing bytecode.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>JVM</b><br>executes bytecode</div></div>'},{caption:'JRE: studio + actor + props.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>JRE</b><br>JVM + libraries</div></div>'},{caption:'JDK: script tools + whole studio.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>JDK</b><br>JRE + javac + tools</div></div>'}]}},
'setup':{intro:'Time to cook for real: install JDK 17, write Main.java, compile, run. Five minutes of setup pays off for the entire course — this exact loop is every lesson from here on.',question:'What is the smallest possible victory that proves your whole kitchen works?',analogy:'The first pancake. It may look odd, but flipping one proves the heat, the pan, and the batter all at once. Hello World proves your editor, compiler, and runner all at once.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: pancakes get eaten and forgotten. Hello World gets kept — you will repeat this exact save → compile → run workflow for every program in this course.',explain:['The ritual: 1 install JDK 17, 2 verify with <b>java -version</b>, 3 create <b>Main.java</b>, 4 run <b>javac Main.java</b> (produces <b>Main.class</b>), 5 run <b>java Main</b>.','The file name must match the public class: class <b>Main</b> lives in <b>Main.java</b>, case-sensitive. <b>Hello</b> in <b>hello.java</b> fails — Java never forgives case.','<b>javac</b> wants the FILE (Main.java); <b>java</b> wants the CLASS (Main, no extension). Mixing them up is every beginner’s first error and this lesson vaccinates you.','Read errors top-first: fix error #1 and rebuild — later errors are often ghosts caused by the first. A “semicolon expected” error points at the exact line missing it.'],tech:['<b>javac Main.java</b> produces <b>Main.class</b> (bytecode). <b>java Main</b> starts the JVM, which loads that class and calls its main.','Java is <b>case-sensitive everywhere</b>: Main, main, and MAIN are three different names.','If commands are “not found”, your shell’s <b>PATH</b> lacks the JDK’s bin folder — reinstall with the “add to PATH” option or set JAVA_HOME.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println("My kitchen works!");\n  }\n}\n// Then in the terminal:\n// javac Main.java\n// java Main`,codeNotes:['Save EXACTLY as Main.java — capitals matter.','javac translates it into Main.class.','java Main executes it and prints the line.'],output:'My kitchen works!',why:'Clean compile → valid Main.class; the JVM loaded it, entered main, ran println. The full loop works end to end.',sim:{kind:'concat',label:'Simulate success: join your name to the greeting.',fields:[{v:'My kitchen works, '},{v:'Ada!'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'File named main.java (lowercase m).',why:'Java demands Main == Main.java — case included.',fix:'Rename the file to Main.java exactly.'},{bad:'Running javac Main (no .java) or java Main.java (with it).',why:'javac wants the FILE; java wants the CLASS. Backwards both ways.',fix:'javac Main.java, then java Main. Tattoo it.'},{bad:'A “semicolon expected” error you ignore.',why:'Translation is all-or-nothing; one typo, zero program.',fix:'Go to the line number it names, add the semicolon, rebuild.'}],practices:[{title:'Order the ritual',task:'Put in order: A) java Main B) javac Main.java C) save Main.java.',hint:'Write → build → run.',sol:'C → B → A. Every time, for months.'},{title:'Break it on purpose',task:'Delete one semicolon, run javac. What is the FIRST error line? Put the semicolon back.',hint:'Read only the first error; ignore the rest.',sol:'An error saying a semicolon is expected, pointing at the broken line. Later errors vanish once it is fixed.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'The correct workflow is…',options:[{t:'Save → javac → java',ok:true},{t:'java → javac → save'},{t:'javac → save → java'},{t:'Only java, no saving'}],explain:'Write it, build it, run it — that order, always.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'The file/class naming rule?',options:[{t:'Public class Main must live in Main.java',ok:true},{t:'Any file name works'},{t:'The file must be .txt'},{t:'Class names must be lowercase'}],explain:'Exact match, case-sensitive, or compilation fails.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'javac reports five errors. You should…',options:[{t:'Fix the first error and rebuild',ok:true},{t:'Ignore them and run java anyway'},{t:'Reinstall everything'},{t:'Delete the file'}],explain:'Error #1 frequently causes #2–#5 as ghosts.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'java Main.java (with extension) fails because…',options:[{t:'java wants a class name, not a file',ok:true},{t:'Main.java is too long'},{t:'Java cannot print'},{t:'The file is empty'}],explain:'javac takes files; java takes classes. Different tools, different arguments.'}],takeaways:['Install JDK 17; verify both version commands.','Save → javac → java, every time.','File name = class name, exactly, case included.','javac takes files, java takes classes.'],recall:'What two commands turn Main.java into visible output, and what does each consume?',todo:'Actually run Hello World on your own machine right now — no reading ahead until it prints.',anim:{title:'Your first build',steps:[{caption:'1 Write Main.java in an editor.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>Main.java</b><br>println(...)</div></div>'},{caption:'2 javac translates it to Main.class.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>javac</b></div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>Main.class</b></div></div>'},{caption:'3 java runs it. Output appears!',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>java Main</b></div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>My kitchen works!</b></div></div>'}]}},
'first-program':{intro:'Every Java program wears the same uniform: a class shell and a main door. Learn the uniform once and you will recognize — and write — every program in this course.',question:'Why do all formal letters start with “Dear…” and end with a signature?',analogy:'Envelope plus letter. The class is the envelope with your name on it (Main). main is the big arrow saying “start reading HERE”. The JVM always opens the envelope exactly at main.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: you can start reading a letter anywhere. The JVM cannot — it demands precisely main(String[] args). Close cousins like Main or main() with no args are rejected.',explain:['<b>public class Main</b> names the blueprint; the file must be Main.java. Everything the program owns lives inside its braces.','<b>public static void main(String[] args)</b> is the start door. Each word has a technical meaning you will earn over the course — for now, memorize its exact shape.','Statements end with <b>;</b> and braces <b>{ }</b> group code into blocks. <b>System.out.println</b> prints text plus a newline. The JVM runs main top-down, line by line.','Later you will learn what public, static, void, and String[] args each mean (methods, scope, arrays). For now they are the magic words that open the door — spelled exactly.'],tech:['<b>public</b> = visible to the JVM launcher. <b>static</b> = runnable without building an object first. <b>void</b> = returns nothing. <b>String[] args</b> = command-line words handed to your program.','The entry signature must match <b>exactly</b>: public static void main(String[] args). Even Main (capital M) fails the search.','One file can hold many classes, but at most one <b>public</b> class — and the file is named after it.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println("Hello, Java!");\n    System.out.println("I am learning.");\n    System.out.println("Line by line.");\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['Line 1: the envelope, named Main.','Line 2: the start-here door, exact shape.','Lines 3–5: three instructions, each ending with a semicolon, running top-down.'],output:'Hello, Java!\nI am learning.\nLine by line.',why:'The JVM entered main, ran line 3 (print), then 4, then 5, then exited. Order is strictly top-down — no skipping, no guessing.',sim:{kind:'concat',label:'Predict output: join two lines.',fields:[{v:'Hello, '},{v:'Java!'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'main spelled Main, or String[] args missing.',why:'The JVM searches for one exact signature and accepts no substitutes.',fix:'Copy precisely: public static void main(String[] args).'},{bad:'A forgotten semicolon at a line end.',why:'Without it Java cannot tell where the instruction ends.',fix:'End every statement with a semicolon.'},{bad:'File Main.java but class main (lowercase).',why:'Case mismatch: the envelope name does not match the file.',fix:'Match exactly: Main inside Main.java.'}],practices:[{title:'Label the uniform',task:'In the code above, point at: the envelope, the door, and one instruction.',hint:'class line / main line / println line.',sol:'Envelope: public class Main. Door: the main line. Instruction: any println line.'},{title:'Add a fourth line',task:'Print your own name as a fourth line. Exactly where does it go?',hint:'Inside main, before its closing brace.',sol:'Add System.out.println("Ada"); after the third println, still inside main.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'Where does the JVM start executing?',options:[{t:'main(String[] args)',ok:true},{t:'Any line it likes'},{t:'The last line'},{t:'Inside comments'}],explain:'One fixed entry point — that is what makes programs predictable.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'What terminates a Java statement?',options:[{t:'A semicolon',ok:true},{t:'A period'},{t:'A new paragraph'},{t:'An emoji'}],explain:'The semicolon marks “instruction complete”.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'Public class Main must live in…',options:[{t:'Main.java',ok:true},{t:'main.java'},{t:'Main.txt'},{t:'Anything you like'}],explain:'Exact match, case-sensitive — the oldest rule in Java.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'What does static mean in the main line (preview)?',options:[{t:'Runnable without building an object first',ok:true},{t:'Unchangeable forever'},{t:'Invisible to others'},{t:'Runs twice'}],explain:'static here = no object needed to enter. (Unchangeable is final — a later lesson.)'}],takeaways:['Class = envelope; main = start door.','Statements end with semicolons; braces group.','The JVM runs main strictly top-down.','Memorize the main shape exactly.'],recall:'Write the main method header from memory, then check.',todo:'Type the program by hand — do not copy-paste — and run it.',anim:{title:'Entering main',steps:[{caption:'The JVM finds the envelope Main.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>Main.class</b><br>envelope</div></div>'},{caption:'It opens the door: main.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>main</b><br>start here →</div></div>'},{caption:'It runs each println top-down.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">Hello, Java!</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box">I am learning.</div></div>'}]}},
'printing':{intro:'Printing is your X-ray into the program: show values, test guesses, and produce friendly output. Ten minutes here pays off in every debugging session forever.',question:'How do you check what is inside a wrapped box without cracking open your program’s skull?',analogy:'A label printer. println prints a label and feeds a fresh line; print stays on the same line; printf fills in a form with blanks (“name: ___, age: ___”) so numbers line up.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: label printers use ink, fonts, and colors. println speaks plain text only — no colors, no bold, no layout beyond characters and line breaks.',explain:['<b>println</b> prints then moves to a new line. <b>print</b> stays on the current line. Combine them to build exact layouts.','Text goes in <b>double quotes</b>; numbers stand bare. The <b>+</b> operator joins pieces left to right — and the moment one side is a String, everything becomes text.','<b>printf</b> formats with placeholders: <b>%d</b> integers, <b>%f</b> decimals (%.2f = two places), <b>%s</b> text, <b>%n</b> newline. It does NOT auto-break lines — end with %n.','Evaluation order bites: "Age: " + 18 + 2 makes "Age: 182" (text from the first +), while "Age: " + (18 + 2) makes "Age: 20". Brackets first, always.'],tech:['<b>System.out</b> is the standard output stream; println/print/printf are methods on a PrintStream object. Errors go to <b>System.err</b> instead.','<b>%.2f</b> rounds (2.555 → 2.56); <b>%d</b> with a double crashes (use %f); <b>%%</b> prints a literal percent sign.','<b>%n</b> is the portable newline (Windows vs Linux differ); avoid hard-coding \\n in printf.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println("Apples: " + 3);\n    System.out.print("Total: ");\n    System.out.println(3 * 2);\n    System.out.printf("Price: $%.2f%n", 2.5);\n    System.out.printf("%s is %d years old%n", "Ada", 18);\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['Line 3 joins text + number into “Apples: 3”.','Lines 4–5 share one line: “Total: 6”.','Line 6 formats 2.5 as $2.50; line 7 mixes %s and %d.'],output:'Apples: 3\nTotal: 6\nPrice: $2.50\nAda is 18 years old',why:'“+” with a String builds text. 3*2 computes to 6 on the still-open print line. printf fills each placeholder in order and rounds 2.5 to two decimals.',sim:{kind:'concat',label:'Build a label: text + number.',fields:[{v:'Apples: '},{v:'3'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'println("3 + 2") and expecting 5.',why:'Quotes mean literal text — the computer prints exactly what is inside.',fix:'Drop the quotes to compute: println(3 + 2); // 5'},{bad:'Forgetting the + between pieces: println("Apples: " 3).',why:'Java cannot glue by placing things side by side.',fix:'"Apples: " + 3 — the plus is the glue.'},{bad:'printf with no %n, then wondering why the next output sticks.',why:'printf never breaks the line by itself.',fix:'End every printf format with %n.'},{bad:'"Age: " + 18 + 2 giving “Age: 182”.',why:'Left-to-right: ("Age: " + 18) is text, then + 2 glues more text.',fix:'"Age: " + (18 + 2) — brackets compute first.'}],practices:[{title:'Predict it',task:'What prints? System.out.print("A"); System.out.println("B"); System.out.println("C");',hint:'print stays, println breaks.',sol:'AB on line one, then C on line two.'},{title:'Format money',task:'Print the value 19.9 as $19.90 using printf.',hint:'%.2f plus %n.',sol:'System.out.printf("$%.2f%n", 19.9);'},{title:'Fix the age line',task:'Fix so it prints “Age: 20”: System.out.println("Age: " + 18 + 2);',hint:'Brackets.',sol:'System.out.println("Age: " + (18 + 2));'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'println vs print?',options:[{t:'println breaks the line; print stays on it',ok:true},{t:'print is just faster to type'},{t:'They are identical'},{t:'print deletes text'}],explain:'The “ln” literally means “line”: print + newline.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'System.out.println(3 + 2) prints…',options:[{t:'5',ok:true},{t:'3 + 2'},{t:'32'},{t:'A compiler error'}],explain:'No quotes → real arithmetic happens first.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'%.2f formats…',options:[{t:'A decimal rounded to 2 places',ok:true},{t:'An integer'},{t:'Centered text'},{t:'A calendar date'}],explain:'f = floating point, .2 = two places after the dot.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'"Total: " + 2 + 3 prints…',options:[{t:'Total: 23',ok:true},{t:'Total: 5'},{t:'Total: 2 + 3'},{t:'A compiler error'}],explain:'Left-to-right text gluing: ("Total: "+2) is text, then +3 glues again.'}],takeaways:['println breaks the line; print does not.','Plus joins text left-to-right; brackets compute first.','printf formats with %d, %.2f, %s — end with %n.'],recall:'When would you deliberately pick print over println?',todo:'Print a tiny shop receipt: 3 item lines plus one formatted total.',anim:{title:'Print heads',steps:[{caption:'print stays on the line, cursor waiting.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>print("Total: ")</b><br>cursor waits…</div></div>'},{caption:'println feeds a fresh line.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>println(6)</b><br>Total: 6 ⏎</div></div>'},{caption:'printf fills blanks precisely.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>$%.2f</b><br>2.5 → 2.50</div></div>'}]}},
'comments':{intro:'Code tells WHAT the machine does; comments tell WHY a human chose it. Future you — debugging at midnight — will thank present you.',question:'Why do museums put little plaques next to paintings instead of letting the art speak?',analogy:'Museum plaque. The painting (code) shows what is there; the plaque (comment) explains why it matters and what to notice. Nobody plaques every brushstroke — and nobody should comment every line.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: plaques never repaint themselves. Comments never execute — a stale comment that contradicts the code actively misleads, which is worse than silence.',explain:['<b>//</b> comments out the rest of one line. <b>/* … */</b> spans many lines. <b>/** … */</b> is documentation the javadoc tool turns into web pages.','Comment the <b>why</b>, never the what: “law in our town” earns its place; “add 1” merely repeats the code.','Clear names beat comments every time: <b>adultAge</b> needs no help, while <b>x2</b> needs rescue. When a comment explains WHAT, rename instead.','Comments are also light switches: beginners temporarily disable code by commenting it out while testing. Delete such corpses before finishing — version control remembers.'],tech:['The compiler strips comments <b>before</b> checking code — they cannot affect behavior, speed, or output in any way.','<b>Javadoc</b> comments (/** … */) with tags like @param and @return generate API docs; libraries live on them.','Commenting out code as “backup” rots within days. Real backup is a commit — delete the corpse.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    // WHY 18? It is the legal adult age in our town.\n    int adultAge = 18;\n    /* Prices below include tax.\n       Change TAX_RATE in one place only. */\n    System.out.println("Adult at " + adultAge);\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['The // comment explains the rule’s source — the why.','The block comment warns about the tax design.','The code itself stays clean and readable.'],output:'Adult at 18',why:'Comments vanished during compilation; only the println ran. Readers still learn the rule behind the number.',sim:{kind:'concat',label:'Comments change nothing — the output stays.',fields:[{v:'18'},{v:' (comment ignored)'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'x++; // adds one to x',why:'Restates the obvious — pure noise that trains readers to ignore ALL your comments.',fix:'Explain the why: // retry count resets after logout.'},{bad:'A stale comment: “max 5” while the code allows 10.',why:'Contradicting prose is worse than silence — readers trust it and break things.',fix:'Update the comment with the code, or delete it.'},{bad:'Commented-out code kept as “backup” for months.',why:'It rots, confuses, and nobody dares delete it.',fix:'Delete it. Git remembers everything.'}],practices:[{title:'Keep or kill?',task:'Which comment helps? A) // increment i, i++ B) // retry 3 times: the server drops the first ping.',hint:'Why beats what.',sol:'B helps — it carries knowledge the code cannot show. A restates the visible.'},{title:'Name it well',task:'Rename int d (days until the trip) so well that no comment is needed.',hint:'Full words, camelCase.',sol:'int daysUntilTrip; — the name IS the documentation.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'Which comment style covers one line?',options:[{t:'//',ok:true},{t:'/* */'},{t:'<!-- -->'},{t:'#'}],explain:'Double-slash runs to the end of the line.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'The best use of a comment is…',options:[{t:'Why this rule or value exists',ok:true},{t:'Restating what the code says'},{t:'Your lunch plans'},{t:'Backing up deleted code'}],explain:'Why survives; what is already visible on screen.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'Do comments change the running program?',options:[{t:'No — stripped before compilation finishes',ok:true},{t:'Yes, they speed it up'},{t:'Yes, they print'},{t:'Only on Fridays'}],explain:'For humans only, always.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'You find code commented out six months ago. Best move?',options:[{t:'Delete it (version control remembers)',ok:true},{t:'Keep it forever, just in case'},{t:'Uncomment it all at once'},{t:'Copy it to another file'}],explain:'Dead code is a liability; history lives in git, not in comments.'}],takeaways:['// line, /* */ block, /** */ documentation.','Comment WHY; let names say WHAT.','Stale comments are bugs written in prose.','Commented-out code is not backup — delete it.'],recall:'What should a comment explain that code fundamentally cannot?',todo:'Add one genuine why-comment to your own Hello program.',anim:{title:'Plaque vs painting',steps:[{caption:'Code paints (it runs).',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>println(18)</b><br>paints 18</div></div>'},{caption:'Comment plaques (the JVM skips them).',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>// law: 18</b><br>for humans</div></div>'},{caption:'Together: clear and correct.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>adultAge = 18</b><br>+ why note</div></div>'}]}},
'variables':{intro:'A variable is a labelled lunchbox: one box, one kind of food, one name tag. Put things in, peek at them, swap them — the tag stays, the content changes.',question:'If your desk had 100 unlabeled drawers, how fast could you find your charger in the dark?',analogy:'Labelled lunchboxes on a shelf. The type is the “soup only” sticker, the name is “Aarav’s” tag, the value is today’s soup. Swap the soup any day; the sticker and tag stay put.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: real boxes accept anything. A Java box accepts exactly ONE declared type — a soup box refuses sandwiches, and the compiler is the canteen auntie enforcing it.',explain:['Declare with type, name, and a starting value: <b>int age = 18;</b> The <b>=</b> STORES (assignment) — it does not ask a question. Questions come later with ==.','Names start with a letter, use <b>camelCase</b> (userAge), say what they hold, and are case-sensitive: age and Age are different boxes.','Reassign any time: <b>age = 19;</b> throws away the old value and stores the new one — one box, one current value, always.','Every variable has exactly three parts — type, name, value — and the right side of = always computes FIRST, then stores. In x = x + 3, the old x is read, 3 is added, and the result moves back into x.'],tech:['Local variables MUST be <b>initialized before use</b> — Java refuses to guess, and the compiler rejects reading an empty box. (Fields get defaults; locals do not.)','Legal names: letters, digits, <b>_</b> and <b>$</b>; cannot start with a digit; cannot be a keyword like int or class. Convention: camelCase, never snake_case.','<b>int</b> is the default integer choice; reach for long/double only with a reason — a theme the types lesson makes precise.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int age = 17;            // declare + initialize\n    System.out.println(age); // 17\n    age = 18;                // birthday: replace the content\n    System.out.println(age); // 18\n    age = age + 1;           // read, add, store back\n    System.out.println(age); // 19\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['Line 3 creates box age holding 17.','Line 5 overwrites it with 18 — 17 is gone.','Line 7 reads 18, adds 1, stores 19 back.'],output:'17\n18\n19',why:'First print reads 17. Assignment overwrites with 18. The last line computes 18 + 1 and stores 19, which the final print shows.',sim:{kind:'arith',label:'Simulate a box: compute a new value to store.',fields:[{v:'17'},{o:['+']},{v:'1'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'int age; then printing age.',why:'An empty local box — Java refuses to guess and the compiler stops you.',fix:'Always initialize: int age = 0; then use it.'},{bad:'age = "old"; when age is int.',why:'Wrong food in the box: text cannot live in an int.',fix:'Keep the type: age = 19; — or declare a String box instead.'},{bad:'Writing = when you mean ==.',why:'Single = stores; double == compares. Different jobs, different symbols.',fix:'Store with =, compare with == (full lesson soon).'}],practices:[{title:'Trace it',task:'int x = 2; x = x + 3; print x. What shows, and why?',hint:'Right side computes first, then stores.',sol:'5 — x + 3 reads 2, makes 5, stores it back into x.'},{title:'Name them',task:'Name and declare boxes for: the user’s city and their game score, with starting values.',hint:'camelCase; types String and int.',sol:'String userCity = "Pune"; int score = 0;'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'int age = 18 means…',options:[{t:'Create an int box named age holding 18',ok:true},{t:'Check whether age equals 18'},{t:'Create 18 boxes'},{t:'Delete age'}],explain:'Declare + initialize in a single line.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'After age = 19, the old value 17…',options:[{t:'Is gone, replaced',ok:true},{t:'Stays alongside the 19'},{t:'Doubles itself'},{t:'Moves to another box'}],explain:'One box holds exactly one current value.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'Why must locals be initialized before use?',options:[{t:'Java will not guess; the compiler rejects empty boxes',ok:true},{t:'It runs faster'},{t:'Boxes look nicer'},{t:'Printers require it'}],explain:'Definite assignment is a compile-time rule for locals.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'x = x + 3 with x starting at 2 gives…',options:[{t:'5',ok:true},{t:'23'},{t:'x + 3 (text)'},{t:'A compiler error'}],explain:'Read 2, add 3, store 5 back.'}],takeaways:['Variable = type + name + value.','Single = stores; names are camelCase and case-sensitive.','One box holds one current value; right side computes first.','Locals must be initialized before reading.'],recall:'What three parts does every variable have, and which computes first in x = x + 3?',todo:'Store your age and city in variables and print both in one friendly line.',anim:{title:'Lunchbox assignment',steps:[{caption:'Declare: a fresh labelled box holding 17.',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">int age</div><div class="vl">17</div></div></div>'},{caption:'Read: peek without emptying the box.',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">age</div><div class="vl">17 → print</div></div></div>'},{caption:'Update: swap in 18. The old value is gone.',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">age</div><div class="vl">18 ✨</div></div></div>'}]}},
'primitives':{intro:'Java ships exactly 8 built-in value types — no more, no less. Pick the smallest box that safely fits your data; in practice that means int, double, boolean, and char carry 95% of beginner code.',question:'Why do shops sell milk in 250 ml, 500 ml, and 1 L instead of one giant tanker per customer?',analogy:'Measuring cups. byte is the espresso shot, int the everyday mug, long the bucket; float and double are graduated cylinders of different precision; char is a single letter tile; boolean is a light switch.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: cups spill visibly and loudly. Numeric overflow wraps SILENTLY — byte 127 + 1 becomes -128 with no warning. Undersized boxes corrupt data instead of complaining.',explain:['Integers: <b>byte</b> (±128), <b>short</b> (±32 thousand), <b>int</b> (±2.1 billion, the default), <b>long</b> (about ±9.2 × 10¹⁸, needs an L suffix). Bigger box, bigger range, more memory.','Decimals: <b>float</b> (~7 trustworthy digits, needs an f suffix) vs <b>double</b> (~15–16 digits, the default for 3.5). Money and science want double; float is for special cases like graphics.','<b>char</b> holds one UTF-16 code unit in single quotes — (int) ’A’ is 65 underneath. <b>boolean</b> is exactly true or false, nothing else, no 0/1 tricks like C.','Naked numbers have default types: 5 is int, 3.5 is double, true is boolean. Suffixes override: 5L is long, 3.5f is float, ’a’ is char.'],tech:['Exact ranges: byte −128..127, short −32,768..32,767, int −2,147,483,648..2,147,483,647, long ≈ ±9.2×10¹⁸, char 0..65,535.','<b>long big = 8000000000;</b> FAILS — the literal is int-sized first, so it overflows before assignment. Always write 8000000000L.','<b>float t = 36.6;</b> FAILS — 36.6 is double and Java never silently squeezes it. Write 36.6f or use double.','A char is secretly a number: ’A’+1 is ’B’, and (int)’A’ is 65. Emoji mostly need TWO chars (surrogate pairs) — char is a UTF-16 unit, not “one symbol”.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int people = 30;\n    long worldPop = 8000000000L;\n    double price = 19.99;\n    float temp = 36.6f;\n    char grade = 'A';\n    boolean passed = true;\n    System.out.println(people);\n    System.out.println(worldPop);\n    System.out.println(price + " " + temp + " " + grade + " " + passed);\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['The L marks a long literal; the f marks a float literal.','Bare 3.5-style decimals are always double.','char uses single quotes; boolean prints as true/false.'],output:'30\n8000000000\n19.99 36.6 A true',why:'Each literal fits its declared type exactly; the last line joins everything into text for display.',sim:{kind:'arith',label:'Integers vs decimals: try 5 / 2 as pure int math.',fields:[{v:'5'},{o:['/']},{v:'2'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'long x = 8000000000; with no L.',why:'The bare literal is int — it overflows BEFORE the assignment happens.',fix:'long x = 8000000000L; — suffix the literal itself.'},{bad:'float t = 36.6; with no f.',why:'36.6 is born double; Java will not squeeze it silently.',fix:'float t = 36.6f; — or simply use double.'},{bad:'char c = "a"; with double quotes.',why:'Double quotes build a String object, never a char.',fix:'char c = ’a’; — singles for tiles, doubles for strings.'},{bad:'byte b = 100; b = b + 1; expecting 101 in a byte.',why:'byte math promotes to int — and 127 + 1 wraps to -128 anyway.',fix:'Use int for math; treat byte/short as storage types.'}],practices:[{title:'Pick types',task:'Choose the best type for each: a person’s age, planet population, pi, a grade letter, a light on/off.',hint:'int, long, double, char, boolean.',sol:'int age; long population; double pi; char grade; boolean on.'},{title:'Char arithmetic',task:'What does System.out.println((int) ’A’); print? Guess, then run it.',hint:'Every char hides a number underneath.',sol:'65 — the UTF-16 code unit for capital A.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'The default type of 3.5 is…',options:[{t:'double',ok:true},{t:'float'},{t:'int'},{t:'long'}],explain:'Bare decimals are born double; float needs the f suffix.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'Which declaration NEEDS a suffix to compile?',options:[{t:'long big = 8000000000L',ok:true},{t:'int n = 5'},{t:'boolean b = true'},{t:'char c = ’x’'}],explain:'Huge longs overflow int range without L; the rest are already exact.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'byte b = 127; then b + 1 behaves as…',options:[{t:'-128 (overflow wraps silently)',ok:true},{t:'128, safely'},{t:'A compiler error'},{t:'Exactly 0'}],explain:'Tiny types wrap around — size the box before the math.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'(int) ’A’ gives 65 because…',options:[{t:'char stores a UTF-16 number underneath',ok:true},{t:'A is the 65th letter typed'},{t:'Casting adds 64'},{t:'65 is random'}],explain:'Chars ARE numbers wearing letter costumes.'}],takeaways:['8 primitives; bare integers default to int, decimals to double.','L marks long, f marks float, single quotes mark char.','Overflow wraps silently — size the box before the math.','char is a UTF-16 number; boolean is strictly true/false.'],recall:'Name all 8 primitives plus each one’s rough range, without peeking.',todo:'Declare one variable of each of int, double, char, boolean and print them with labels.',anim:{title:'Sizing boxes',steps:[{caption:'byte: the espresso shot (−128..127).',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">byte</div><div class="vl">127</div></div></div>'},{caption:'int: the everyday mug (±2.1 billion).',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">int</div><div class="vl">2,147,483,647</div></div></div>'},{caption:'Overflow: one sip too many wraps to −128.',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">byte+1</div><div class="vl">-128 ⚠️</div></div></div>'}]}},
'references':{intro:'Primitives hold values directly. References hold DIRECTIONS to objects — like a home address slip, not the house itself. This single distinction explains half of Java’s trickiest beginner errors.',question:'If you photocopy a house key, have you photocopied the house?',analogy:'House addresses. Two slips can point at one house (sharing). Primitives are cash in hand; references are address slips to houses (objects) sitting in a district called the heap.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: addresses never build houses — somebody must construct first. A slip reading null points at an empty lot, and “visiting” it crashes with NullPointerException.',explain:['A primitive like <b>int x = 5</b> holds 5 itself. A reference like <b>String s</b> holds directions to a text object living elsewhere in memory.','<b>new</b> builds the house: new StringBuilder("hi") constructs a fresh object. Without new, a reference may hold <b>null</b> — directions to nowhere.','Copying a reference copies the SLIP, not the house: Book b2 = b1 means one book with two slips. Renovate through one slip, see it through both.','This is why == on objects usually lies to beginners: it compares the SLIPS (same house?), not the houses (same furniture?). Content comparison needs .equals() — the Strings lesson makes this muscle memory.'],tech:['Objects live on the <b>heap</b>; local variable slots (primitives AND reference slips) live on the <b>stack</b>. The slip is typically one machine word.','Calling any method on <b>null</b> throws <b>NullPointerException</b> — Java’s most famous crash. Guard with if (s != null).','Default values: object fields start as <b>null</b>, numbers as 0, booleans as false. Local variables get NO defaults — initialize them.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    String a = "hello";\n    String b = a;  // second slip, SAME house\n    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("hi");\n    sb.append("!"); // renovate the shared house\n    System.out.println(a);\n    System.out.println(b);\n    System.out.println(sb);\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['b = a copies only the slip — one shared string.','new builds a genuinely fresh builder house.','append mutates that house to “hi!”.'],output:'hello\nhello\nhi!',why:'a and b point at one shared string, so both print hello. sb points at a builder we renovated in place to hi!.',sim:{kind:'concat',label:'Sharing: two names, one text.',fields:[{v:'hello'},{v:' (shared)'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'“The reference CONTAINS the whole object.”',why:'The slip is tiny; the house lives on the heap. Believing otherwise makes sharing bugs invisible.',fix:'Always say: s POINTS TO a String.'},{bad:'Calling s.length() when s could be null.',why:'There is no house at null — instant NullPointerException.',fix:'Guard first: if (s != null) { ... }'},{bad:'Expecting Book b2 = b1 to clone the book.',why:'Only the slip was copied; both slips share one house.',fix:'To truly clone, construct: new Book(...) with copied values.'}],practices:[{title:'Shared or copied?',task:'int x = 5; int y = x; x = 9. What is y now? Then: String a = "h"; String c = a. Same idea or different?',hint:'Primitives copy the VALUE; references copy the DIRECTION.',sol:'y stays 5 (value copied). c shares the string house (direction copied).'},{title:'Write a null guard',task:'Write a safe length computation that returns 0 when s is null.',hint:'Check before touching.',sol:'if (s == null) return 0; return s.length();'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'A reference variable holds…',options:[{t:'Directions to an object (or null)',ok:true},{t:'The entire object inside itself'},{t:'Only numbers'},{t:'Nothing, ever'}],explain:'An address slip, never the house.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'Book b2 = b1 means…',options:[{t:'One book with two references',ok:true},{t:'Two separate books'},{t:'Zero books'},{t:'A compiler error'}],explain:'The slip copied; the house is shared.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'Calling a method on null produces…',options:[{t:'NullPointerException',ok:true},{t:'A bigger house'},{t:'Zero, quietly'},{t:'Nothing — it is safe'}],explain:'Nowhere to visit means an immediate crash.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'Where do objects vs local slips live?',options:[{t:'Objects on the heap, local slots on the stack',ok:true},{t:'Everything on the stack'},{t:'Everything on the disk'},{t:'Inside the CPU'}],explain:'Houses in heap-district; slips in the stack-pocket.'}],takeaways:['Primitives hold values; references point at objects.','new builds; null points nowhere — guard it.','Copying a reference shares the object.','== compares slips; .equals compares content.'],recall:'Cash versus address slip: which one is the primitive, and what happens when you copy each?',todo:'Draw boxes-and-arrows on paper for String a plus b = a, then for sb.',anim:{title:'Slips and houses',steps:[{caption:'Primitive: cash held in hand.',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">int x</div><div class="vl">5</div></div></div>'},{caption:'Reference: a slip pointing at a house.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>s</b><br>slip ➜</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>"hello" 🏠</b><br>object</div></div>'},{caption:'b = a: two slips, ONE house.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>a ➜ 🏠</b></div><div class="box"><b>b ➜ 🏠</b></div></div>'}]}},
'literals':{intro:'Literals are values typed directly into code: 42, 3.5, single letters, quoted text, true. Constants (final) give important ones names, so magic numbers stop haunting the codebase.',question:'Why do buildings label doors “EXIT” instead of painting mysterious green rectangles everywhere?',analogy:'Name tags versus sticky notes. The number 3.14159 scribbled in twenty places is twenty sticky notes that rot separately. final double PI = 3.14159 is one engraved tag — a single truth, reused safely.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: engraved tags cannot be re-engraved. final is a compiler-enforced promise — reassigning it breaks the build ON PURPOSE, loudly, at once.',explain:['Integer literals are <b>int</b> by birth (add <b>L</b> for long). Decimal literals are <b>double</b> (add <b>f</b> for float). Single quotes make char, double quotes make String, and 1_000_000 shows underscores are human-only decoration.','<b>final double PI = 3.14159;</b> names a constant: assigned exactly once, then locked. Convention writes constants in <b>UPPER_CASE</b> so they visually shout “do not touch”.','Char and escape details: ’\\n’ is newline, ’\\t’ is tab, ’\\\\’ is one backslash. Inside "..." the same escapes work, so "a\\nb" prints two lines.','Java 17 also offers <b>text blocks</b> (“""...""”) for multi-line strings — HTML, SQL, JSON without plus-chains. Preview now, master later.'],tech:['Underscores may sit BETWEEN digits only: 1_000 ok, _100 and 100_ are illegal. Bases: 0b101 (binary), 017 (octal!), 0xFF (hex) — note the sneaky leading-zero octal.','<b>final</b> on a reference locks the SLIP, not the house: final StringBuilder sb can still append. Immutability and final-ness are different ideas.','A <b>magic number</b> is any unexplained literal in logic. Name it, and the code starts documenting itself.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    final double PI = 3.14159; // engraved tag\n    int count = 1000;\n    long big = 5000000000L;\n    double circle = PI * 2 * 2; // 2πr with r = 2\n    System.out.println("PI = " + PI);\n    System.out.println("count = " + count);\n    System.out.println("circle = " + circle);\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['PI is named once, then reused — change it in one place.','The L suffix makes the huge literal a long.','circle computes 3.14159 × 4 = 12.56636.'],output:'PI = 3.14159\ncount = 1000\ncircle = 12.56636',why:'Named constant keeps every use consistent; the math is plain 2πr with r = 2.',sim:{kind:'arith',label:'Constants keep math honest. Try radius math.',fields:[{v:'3.14159'},{o:['*']},{v:'4'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'The same 0.18 tax rate scattered across 20 lines.',why:'One law change means 20 edits — and you WILL miss one.',fix:'final double TAX_RATE = 0.18; then reuse TAX_RATE everywhere.'},{bad:'PI = 3.0; after declaring it final.',why:'final means assigned-once; the compiler rejects the betrayal.',fix:'Remove final (if it must vary) or keep the single assignment.'},{bad:'Expecting final to freeze an object’s insides.',why:'final locks the slip. final StringBuilder sb = ...; sb.append("x"); is perfectly legal.',fix:'Want frozen content? Use String (immutable) — a later lesson.'}],practices:[{title:'Name the magic',task:'Replace the magic 0.18 in price * 0.18 with a named constant.',hint:'final double TAX_RATE = 0.18;',sol:'final double TAX_RATE = 0.18; then price * TAX_RATE;'} ,{title:'Spot the literal types',task:'Name the type of each: 7, 7L, 7.0, 7.0f, ’7’, "7".',hint:'int, long, double, float, char, String.',sol:'int, long, double, float, char, String — in that order.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'3.5 is which type by default?',options:[{t:'double',ok:true},{t:'float'},{t:'int'},{t:'long'}],explain:'Bare decimals are born double; float demands the f.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'final on a variable means…',options:[{t:'Assigned once, then locked',ok:true},{t:'Secret and hidden'},{t:'Faster to type'},{t:'A bigger number'}],explain:'A single-assignment promise the compiler enforces.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'Single vs double quotes?',options:[{t:'char vs String',ok:true},{t:'Exactly the same'},{t:'String vs char'},{t:'Both are numbers'}],explain:'Singles tile one char; doubles build String objects.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'final StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(); sb.append("x"); — legal?',options:[{t:'Yes — final locks the slip, not the house',ok:true},{t:'No — final freezes everything'},{t:'Only on Fridays'},{t:'Only for Strings'}],explain:'final ≠ immutable. The reference is locked; the object still works.'}],takeaways:['Literals have default types; suffixes override them.','Name magic numbers as UPPER_CASE final constants.','Single quotes char, double quotes String.','final locks the reference, not the object.'],recall:'Which literal needs L, which needs f, and where may underscores sit?',todo:'Find one magic number in your old code and promote it to a final constant.',anim:{title:'Sticky notes become name tags',steps:[{caption:'Magic numbers scattered everywhere.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">0.18 … 0.18 … 0.18</div></div>'},{caption:'One engraved tag instead.',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">TAX_RATE</div><div class="vl">0.18 🔒</div></div></div>'},{caption:'Reuse safely; change exactly once.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">price × TAX_RATE</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box">always right</div></div>'}]}},
'casting':{intro:'Sometimes a big value must squeeze into a small box (narrowing) or a small one joins big math (widening). Java refuses to guess about loss — when information might vanish, YOU sign permission with a cast.',question:'What happens when you pour a bucket into a mug?',analogy:'Funnels. Widening (mug → bucket, int → long) always fits, so Java pours automatically. Narrowing (bucket → mug, double → int) spills — you must sign “I accept the spill” by writing (int).',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: funnels in kitchens roughly preserve; Java casts CHOP. (int) 3.99 is 3, not 4 — truncation toward zero, never rounding. The spill is silent.',explain:['<b>Widening</b> conversions are automatic and safe: byte → short → int → long → float → double, plus char → int. No data can be lost, so no permission slip needed.','<b>Narrowing</b> needs an explicit cast and may mangle: (int) 3.99 → 3, (byte) 130 → -126, (int) -3.9 → -3. The compiler demands you own the loss.','Two classic traps: <b>int/int stays int</b> (5/2 = 2, remainder silently dropped) and <b>byte/short/char arithmetic promotes to int</b> (so byte b = b + 1 does not even compile).','The average bug: double avg = sum / count with ints divides as ints FIRST (truncating), then stores the damage in a double. Cast BEFORE dividing: (double) sum / count.'],tech:['Widening chain: byte → short → int → long → float → double; also char → int. Everything else narrowing needs (type).','<b>Compound assignment casts implicitly</b>: x += 2.5 on an int x compiles (chopping!) while x = x + 2.5 does not. Know the asymmetry.','<b>Math.round(3.9)</b> gives 4 (long); (int) Math.round(3.9) gives int 4. Chop vs round is a deliberate choice.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int a = 5, b = 2;\n    System.out.println(a / b);    // 2: int division chops\n    System.out.println(a / 2.0);  // 2.5: double lifts the math\n    double d = 3.99;\n    int n = (int) d;              // 3: chop, never round\n    System.out.println(n);\n    long rounded = Math.round(d); // 4: real rounding\n    System.out.println(rounded);\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['int/int stays int — the remainder vanishes.','One double operand lifts the whole expression.','(int) chops toward zero; Math.round actually rounds.'],output:'2\n2.5\n3\n4',why:'Integer division truncates before anyone sees decimals. The 2.0 widens the expression to double. The cast chops .99; Math.round earns the 4.',sim:{kind:'arith',label:'Feel truncation: divide two ints.',fields:[{v:'5'},{o:['/']},{v:'2'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'double avg = sum / count; with both int.',why:'The right side truncates to int FIRST — storing it in a double afterwards rescues nothing.',fix:'double avg = (double) sum / count; — cast before dividing.'},{bad:'int n = 3.9; and hoping.',why:'Narrowing without permission is a compile error, full stop.',fix:'int n = (int) 3.9; // 3 — signed, sealed, chopped.'},{bad:'Expecting (int) to round 3.99 to 4.',why:'Casts chop toward zero. Always. 3.99 → 3, -3.99 → -3.',fix:'Round properly: (int) Math.round(3.99); // 4'}],practices:[{title:'Fix the average',task:'int s = 7, c = 2. Produce avg = 3.5, not 3.',hint:'Cast BEFORE the division happens.',sol:'double avg = (double) s / c;'} ,{title:'Predict the chop',task:'What are (int) -3.9 and (byte) 130? Guess, then run.',hint:'Toward zero; bytes wrap at 127.',sol:'-3 and -126. Toward zero, then wrap.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'5 / 2 with both int gives…',options:[{t:'2',ok:true},{t:'2.5'},{t:'3'},{t:'A compiler error'}],explain:'Int division truncates the remainder into oblivion.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'(int) 3.99 gives…',options:[{t:'3',ok:true},{t:'4'},{t:'3.99'},{t:'0'}],explain:'Casts chop toward zero; they never round.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'Which conversion is automatic (widening)?',options:[{t:'int → double',ok:true},{t:'double → int'},{t:'long → byte'},{t:'double → float'}],explain:'Widening always fits, so no permission needed.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'int x = 5; x += 2.5; — compiles? Result?',options:[{t:'Yes, x becomes 7 (chopped)',ok:true},{t:'No, compile error'},{t:'Yes, x becomes 7.5'},{t:'x becomes 8'}],explain:'Compound assignment hides an implicit cast: x = (int)(x + 2.5). Sneaky but true.'}],takeaways:['Widening is automatic; narrowing needs (type).','int/int truncates — cast BEFORE dividing for decimals.','Casts chop toward zero; Math.round rounds.','+= hides a cast; = does not.'],recall:'How do you honestly get 2.5 out of ints 5 and 2?',todo:'Find one division in your old code and check: int or double math?',anim:{title:'Bucket versus mug',steps:[{caption:'Widening: mug into bucket, automatic.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>int 5</b></div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>double 5.0</b> ✓</div></div>'},{caption:'Narrowing: bucket into mug spills.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>3.99</b></div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>(int) 3</b> ⚠️ chop</div></div>'},{caption:'int/int: 5/2 = 2, remainder lost.',html:'<div class="viz"><div class="varbox"><div class="nm">5 / 2</div><div class="vl">2</div></div></div>'}]}},
'operators':{intro:'Operators are verbs: math (+ - * / %), comparison (== != < >), and logic (&& || !). Precedence decides who acts first — and brackets overrule everything.',question:'Why does “2 + 3 × 4” start arguments even among adults?',analogy:'A canteen line with VIPs. × ÷ % stride in first, + − follow, comparisons trail after, && then || last of all. Brackets are the teacher escorting anyone straight to the front.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: VIP order is human convention, not physics. Nobody will praise you for memorizing 15 levels — when unsure, add brackets. Explicit beats clever, every time.',explain:['<b>Arithmetic</b>: + - * / % (remainder). Shorthands <b>+= -= *= /= %=</b> update in place; <b>++ / --</b> step by one.','<b>Comparison</b> (== != < <= > >=) answers boolean. <b>Logic</b> combines: && both-must-hold, || either-holds, ! flips. Comparisons bind tighter than &&, which binds tighter than ||.','<b>&& and || short-circuit</b>: evaluation STOPS the moment the answer is decided. So (s != null && s.length() > 0) is safe — the right side never runs on null.','<b>%</b> keeps the remainder: 7 % 3 = 1, and n % 2 == 0 tests evenness. Its sign follows the LEFT side: -7 % 3 = -1. And prefix ++i vs postfix i++ differ: the expression value is taken before or after the step.'],tech:['Full precedence (high→low): postfix → unary → * / % → + − → comparisons → == != → && → || → assignment. Brackets beat all of it.','<b>int i = 5; int j = i++;</b> → j is 5, i becomes 6. With ++i, j would be 6. Postfix = “use, then step”.','<b>=</b> assigns and itself PRODUCES the value (so a = b = 5 chains). == compares. Mixing them is the classic beginner crash.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int age = 20;\n    boolean teen = age >= 13 && age <= 19;\n    System.out.println(teen); // false: 20 fails the second half\n    System.out.println(7 % 3); // 1: the leftover\n    int x = 5;\n    x += 2; // x = x + 2\n    System.out.println(x); // 7\n    int i = 5;\n    System.out.println(i++); // 5: use-then-step\n    System.out.println(i);   // 6: stepped now\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['&& needs BOTH halves true; 20 is not ≤ 19.','7 % 3 is the leftover after two 3s.','i++ yields 5, THEN steps to 6.'],output:'false\n1\n7\n5\n6',why:'20 fails the teen range; remainder of 7÷3 is 1; x grows to 7; postfix yields-then-steps.',sim:{kind:'arith',label:'Try % (remainder) — perfect for even/odd tests.',fields:[{v:'7'},{o:['%']},{v:'3'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'if (x = 5) with a single =.',why:'That STORES 5 (and yields 5) instead of asking anything — usually a compile error in conditions, always a bug in intent.',fix:'Ask with double: if (x == 5).'},{bad:'Computing 2 + 3 * 4 as 20.',why:'The × VIP goes first: 2 + 12 = 14.',fix:'Want 20? Escort with brackets: (2 + 3) * 4.'},{bad:'Comparing text with ==.',why:'== compares the address slips, not the words — almost always false for distinct objects.',fix:'Compare content: word.equals(other) — full lesson coming.'},{bad:'i++ vs ++i treated as identical.',why:'As statements they match; INSIDE expressions they differ by one. That one bites.',fix:'Keep ++/-- on their own lines until fluent.'}],practices:[{title:'Even or odd?',task:'Write the boolean isEven for an int n, using %.',hint:'Remainder zero means even.',sol:'boolean isEven = (n % 2 == 0);'},{title:'Bracket it',task:'Rewrite 2 + 3 * 4 - 6 / 2 with brackets showing the true order, then compute.',hint:'× and ÷ first.',sol:'2 + (3*4) - (6/2) = 2 + 12 - 3 = 11.'},{title:'Prefix vs postfix',task:'int a = 5; int p = a++; int q = ++a; What are p and q? (a starts 5.)',hint:'Postfix uses-then-steps; prefix steps-then-uses. Track a.',sol:'p = 5 (a→6). q: ++a makes a 7, yields 7. So p=5, q=7.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'7 % 3 equals…',options:[{t:'1',ok:true},{t:'2'},{t:'2.33'},{t:'0'}],explain:'3 + 3 leaves a remainder of 1.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'x += 2 means…',options:[{t:'x = x + 2',ok:true},{t:'x == 2'},{t:'x = 2'},{t:'x + 2, discarded'}],explain:'Update-in-place shorthand.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'a && b is true exactly when…',options:[{t:'Both a and b are true',ok:true},{t:'Either one is true'},{t:'Both are false'},{t:'Always'}],explain:'AND demands both; OR settles for one.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'int i = 5; print(i++) shows… then i is…',options:[{t:'5, then i becomes 6',ok:true},{t:'6, then i stays 6'},{t:'5, and i stays 5'},{t:'6, then i becomes 5'}],explain:'Postfix: yield the old value, THEN step.'}],takeaways:['% keeps remainders — ideal for cycles and parity.','Single = stores, double == compares.','Brackets beat memorizing 15 precedence levels.','Keep ++/-- on their own lines until fluent.'],recall:'What does short-circuit mean for &&, and which null-guard exploits it?',todo:'Write a teen-check for your own age, print it, then test ages 13, 19, 20.',anim:{title:'VIP queue',steps:[{caption:'× ÷ % jump the queue first.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">3 × 4 = 12 (VIP)</div></div>'},{caption:'+ − go next in line.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">2 + 12 = 14</div></div>'},{caption:'Brackets overrule the whole queue.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">(2+3) × 4 = 20 🎓</div></div>'}]}},
'strings':{intro:'Text rules every application — names, messages, files, network. In Java, text is a String OBJECT with superpowers, and + sews pieces into new strings.',question:'How do you sew two ribbons into one longer ribbon?',analogy:'Bead strings. Each String is beads knotted on a thread; + knots two threads into a LONGER NEW thread. The originals hang untouched on the wall (immutability preview!).',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: real knots join the originals permanently. Java’s + builds a brand-new string and leaves both inputs unchanged — plus == compares the KNOTS (identity), not the beads. Content needs .equals().',explain:['Create with doubles: <b>String name = "Ada";</b> Join with +: <b>"Hi " + name</b>. Numbers join too: "Age: " + 18 glues into text.','Evaluation runs LEFT to right: "Age: " + 18 + 2 becomes "Age: 182" (text from the first glue), while "Age: " + (18 + 2) computes the brackets first → "Age: 20".','First tools: <b>length()</b> counts every character including spaces; <b>equals()</b> compares CONTENT (== compares identity!); <b>+</b> builds. The full toolkit — charAt, substring, split — arrives in Stage 4.','Empty vs blank vs missing: <b>""</b> is a real string of length 0; <b>" "</b> holds one space; <b>null</b> is no string at all (and .length() on it crashes). Three different things!'],tech:['Strings are <b>immutable</b>: no method ever edits one in place; “edits” return fresh strings. Loop-building with + is slow — StringBuilder exists for that.','Identical literals may share one pooled object (<b>string pool</b>), which is exactly why == sometimes “works” and fools beginners. Always use equals().','<b>"a" + 1 + 2</b> → "a12" but <b>1 + 2 + "a"</b> → "3a": math happens until the FIRST string appears.'],code:`public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    String name = "Ada";\n    int age = 18;\n    String msg = "Hi " + name + ", age " + age;\n    System.out.println(msg);\n    System.out.println("length = " + msg.length());\n    System.out.println(msg.equals("Hi Ada, age 18"));\n  }\n}`,codeNotes:['+ with a String turns everything into text, left to right.','msg is a brand-new string; the pieces are untouched.','length() counts letters, spaces, comma, digits: 14.'],output:'Hi Ada, age 18\nlength = 14\ntrue',why:'Pieces knot into one new string of length 14; the equals comparison against identical content is true.',sim:{kind:'concat',label:'Sew ribbons: join two texts.',fields:[{v:'Hi '},{v:'Ada'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'if (name == "Ada") to compare words.',why:'== compares identity (same house?) — two equal-content strings are usually different objects.',fix:'Compare content: if (name.equals("Ada")).'},{bad:'"Age: " + 18 + 2 expecting 20.',why:'Left-to-right gluing: ("Age: " + 18) is already text, so + 2 glues more text → "Age: 182".',fix:'Compute first: "Age: " + (18 + 2).'},{bad:'"Hi"+name with no space, printing “HiAda”.',why:'The plus is glue, not a typesetter — it adds exactly what you give it.',fix:'"Hi " + name — supply the space yourself.'},{bad:'Calling methods on a null String.',why:'null is no string at all — .length() on it detonates.',fix:'Guard: if (s != null) ... or default to "".'}],practices:[{title:'Fix the equality',task:'Fix this: if (city == "Pune") { ... }',hint:'Content needs equals.',sol:'if (city.equals("Pune")) { ... }'},{title:'Build a badge',task:'From name and age, build exactly "Ada (18)" with brackets and a space.',hint:'"(" + age + ")" glues the tail.',sol:'String badge = name + " (" + age + ")";'},{title:'Count it',task:'What is "Hi".length() + "!" length math? Careful: + with strings.',hint:'"Hi".length() is int 2 first.',sol:'2 + "!" → "2!" — int first, then glue. Prints 2!.'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'"Hi " + "Ada" produces…',options:[{t:'Hi Ada',ok:true},{t:'HiAda+?'},{t:'A compiler error'},{t:'Hi, Ada, Hi'}],explain:'Plus knots strings into one new string.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'String contents are compared with…',options:[{t:'.equals()',ok:true},{t:'=='},{t:'='},{t:'!='}],explain:'equals checks the beads; == checks whether it is the same knot.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'"Age: " + 18 + 2 gives…',options:[{t:'Age: 182',ok:true},{t:'Age: 20'},{t:'38'},{t:'A compiler error'}],explain:'Left-to-right: text appears at the first glue and never leaves.'},{id:'q4',prompt:'Which statement is TRUE?',options:[{t:'"" has length 0 but is a real object',ok:true},{t:'null has length 0'},{t:'" " has length 0'},{t:'== compares string contents'}],explain:'Empty ≠ blank ≠ missing. Only "" is length-zero.'}],takeaways:['String = text object; + knots a NEW string.','Content compares with equals — never ==.','length() counts every single character.','Empty, blank, and null are three different things.'],recall:'Why does "a" + 1 + 2 differ from 1 + 2 + "a"?',todo:'Print a greeting badge with your name and city, then print its length.',anim:{title:'Knot ribbons',steps:[{caption:'Two ribbons: "Hi " and "Ada".',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">"Hi "</div><div class="box">"Ada"</div></div>'},{caption:'+ knots a BRAND-NEW ribbon.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">"Hi "</div><span class="arr">+</span><div class="box">"Ada"</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box"><b>"Hi Ada"</b> ✨</div></div>'},{caption:'Both originals hang unchanged.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">"Hi " still</div><div class="box">"Ada" still</div></div>'}]}},
'scanner':{intro:'Programs come alive when they LISTEN. Scanner reads keyboard words and numbers — including one famous trap that has bitten every Java beginner, which you will now dodge on purpose.',question:'Why do ATMs ask exactly one question at a time — and wait?',analogy:'A curious cashier with two tools: nextInt grabs a number token off the tray; nextLine scoops the whole sentence tray. After a number, a leftover newline crumb sits on the tray — and the next scoop grabs the crumb instead of your answer.',analogyFail:'Where it breaks: human cashiers forgive and clarify. Scanner never forgives: a wrong-shaped token (abc for a number) detonates with InputMismatchException. Validate, don’t hope.',explain:['Build it on System.in: <b>new Scanner(System.in)</b>. Then <b>nextInt()</b>, <b>nextDouble()</b>, <b>next()</b> (ONE word), <b>nextLine()</b> (the WHOLE line).','THE TRAP: nextInt() eats the number but leaves the <b>\\n</b> behind. The next nextLine() then “reads” that leftover as an empty answer. Fix: one extra <b>nextLine()</b> right after every nextInt/nextDouble to eat the crumb.','Mechanics: Scanner splits input into <b>tokens</b> (whitespace-separated chunks). nextInt demands the next token be integer-shaped; nextLine ignores tokens and grabs to the next newline. Two different worldviews — hence the trap.','Defend: <b>hasNextInt()</b> peeks safely before reading; close with <b>in.close()</b> when truly done (leave System.in open in tiny single-use apps if you like).'],tech:['The newline trap is <b>delimiter</b> mechanics: token methods skip leading whitespace but stop AT the newline without consuming it; nextLine consumes through it.','Wrong token type → <b>InputMismatchException</b> (unchecked — crashes unless handled). hasNextInt()/hasNextDouble() prevent it.','<b>next()</b> stops at ANY whitespace, so “Ada Lovelace” arrives as just “Ada”. Full names need nextLine().'],code:`import java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);\n    System.out.print("Age: ");\n    int age = in.nextInt();\n    in.nextLine(); // eat the leftover newline crumb\n    System.out.print("Name: ");\n    String name = in.nextLine();\n    System.out.println("Hi " + name + ", age " + age);\n    in.close();\n  }\n}\n// Sample run (you type 18 and Ada):\n// Age: 18\n// Name: Ada\n// Hi Ada, age 18`,codeNotes:['nextInt reads the number but abandons the newline.','The extra nextLine eats that crumb.','nextLine then reads the REAL name line.'],output:'Age: 18\nName: Ada\nHi Ada, age 18',why:'Clearing the leftover newline lets the name read correctly instead of swallowing an empty string.',interactive:true,sim:{kind:'concat',label:'Simulate the greeting assembly.',fields:[{v:'Ada'},{v:', 18'}]},mistakes:[{bad:'Name reads as "" — the question gets skipped.',why:'The leftover newline from nextInt was eaten as “the answer”.',fix:'Insert in.nextLine(); immediately after every nextInt()/nextDouble().'},{bad:'Typing abc when nextInt() waits.',why:'Token-shape mismatch → InputMismatchException, program dead.',fix:'Peek first: if (in.hasNextInt()) { ... } else { /* complain kindly */ }'},{bad:'next() for a full name, getting only “Ada”.',why:'next() halts at the first space — “Ada Lovelace” becomes “Ada”.',fix:'Whole sentences need nextLine(), not next().'},{bad:'Closing System.in early, then opening a second Scanner.',why:'Closing the first Scanner closes System.in itself — the second one reads from a corpse.',fix:'One Scanner for the whole program; close once at the very end.'}],practices:[{title:'Fix the skip',task:'Age-then-city code skips the city. Add the one-line fix in the right place.',hint:'Eat the crumb right after the number.',sol:'Insert in.nextLine(); directly after the nextInt() line.'},{title:'Mini Q&A',task:'Ask favorite food (whole line) and rating 1–5 (int). Print “Food: X, 5/5”.',hint:'Number first means trap first.',sol:'Read int, eat newline, read line, print joined — in that order.'},{title:'Guard it',task:'Read an int ONLY if the user actually typed one; else print “digits please”.',hint:'hasNextInt peeks without consuming.',sol:'if (in.hasNextInt()) { int n = in.nextInt(); ... } else { System.out.println("digits please"); }'}],quiz:[{id:'q1',prompt:'After nextInt(), the next nextLine() skips. The fix is…',options:[{t:'One extra nextLine() to eat the leftover newline',ok:true},{t:'Restarting the computer'},{t:'Using only nextInt forever'},{t:'Deleting the Scanner'}],explain:'Consume the abandoned newline crumb first.'},{id:'q2',prompt:'To read the full name “Ada L” you use…',options:[{t:'nextLine()',ok:true},{t:'nextInt()'},{t:'next()'},{t:'nextDouble()'}],explain:'Whole line including spaces — only nextLine does that.'},{id:'q3',prompt:'The user types abc where nextInt() waits. What happens?',options:[{t:'InputMismatchException — a crash',ok:true},{t:'It politely reads abc'},{t:'It silently reads 0'},{t:'Nothing at all'}],explain:'Wrong token shape detonates. Peek with hasNextInt().'},{id:'q4',prompt:'Why does next() return only “Ada” from “Ada Lovelace”?',options:[{t:'It stops at the first whitespace',ok:true},{t:'It deletes surnames'},{t:'Names are too long'},{t:'It reads backwards'}],explain:'next() = one token. Sentences need nextLine().'},{id:'q5',prompt:'Closing one Scanner then opening another on System.in…',options:[{t:'Fails — the first close shut System.in itself',ok:true},{t:'Works perfectly'},{t:'Doubles the speed'},{t:'Reads twice as much'}],explain:'One Scanner per program; close once at the end.'}],takeaways:['Scanner listens: nextInt / nextDouble / next / nextLine.','Eat the newline crumb after every number read.','nextLine for sentences; validate with hasNextX.','One Scanner per program; close once at the end.'],recall:'In one sentence: what exactly does the “extra nextLine” consume, and why is it there?',todo:'Build a 2-question Q&A, run it on a friend, and watch the trap NOT bite.',anim:{title:'The newline trap',steps:[{caption:'You type 18 ⏎: number taken, ⏎ abandoned.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box"><b>Tray</b><br>18 | ⏎ leftover</div></div>'},{caption:'nextLine eats ⏎ → name reads empty. 😱',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">name = "" 😱</div></div>'},{caption:'Fix: an extra nextLine clears the tray first.',html:'<div class="pipe"><div class="box">eat ⏎</div><span class="arr">→</span><div class="box">name = "Ada" ✓</div></div>'}]}},
};
